====================================================================================
 THE COVERING-BOUND CAP AS A PAIR-NORMALIZED PASCAL MASS  cap_k = C(k+1,2)/C(14,2)
====================================================================================
  k |   cap_k (true) |  C(k+1,2)/91 (pair-Pascal) |  dip = tri - cap |   margin 1-cap
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  8 |      2243/5880 |                36/91   dip |       1081/76440 |      3637/5880
  9 |      1979/4004 |                45/91   dip |           1/4004 |      2025/4004
 10 |          55/91 |                55/91 EXACT |                0 |          36/91
 11 |          66/91 |                66/91 EXACT |                0 |          25/91
 12 |            6/7 |                  6/7 EXACT |                0 |            1/7
 13 |              1 |                    1 EXACT |                0 |              0
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 margin 1-cap_k = (91 - C(k+1,2))/91 = # pairs OUTSIDE the (k+1)-block / 91  [exact for k>=10]:
   k= 8: 1-cap=0.61854   pair-complement (91-C(k+1,2))/91 = 55/91 = 0.60440   (dip)
   k= 9: 1-cap=0.50574   pair-complement (91-C(k+1,2))/91 = 46/91 = 0.50549   (dip)
   k=10: 1-cap=0.39560   pair-complement (91-C(k+1,2))/91 = 36/91 = 0.39560   ==
   k=11: 1-cap=0.27473   pair-complement (91-C(k+1,2))/91 = 25/91 = 0.27473   ==
   k=12: 1-cap=0.14286   pair-complement (91-C(k+1,2))/91 = 1/7 = 0.14286   ==
   k=13: 1-cap=0.00000   pair-complement (91-C(k+1,2))/91 = 0 = 0.00000   ==

====================================================================================
 THE DIP = the higher-Pascal (Krawtchouk j>=3) tightening at the SPARSE binding rows
====================================================================================
 The pair-Pascal mass C(k+1,2)/91 is the degree-2 (pairwise) value -- EXACT once the config is
 dense (k>=10). For the sparse rows k=8,9 the true cap dips below it; the dip is the cubic/quartic
 correction = exactly gK8's -9 S3 + 6 S4 (mac-mini S60: k=8 is the S2/S3/S4-balance row).
   dip_9 = 1979/4004 vs 45/91  ->  1/4004 = 1/4004 = 1/(44*91)
   dip_8 = 1081/76440 = 0.014142  (the largest dip = the hardest row)

 PAIR-NORMALIZED PASCAL MASS as a probability (the hypergeometric reading):
   cap_k = P(a uniformly random pair {i,j} of the 14 clock-positions both lie in a (k+1)-block)
         = C(k+1,2)/C(14,2)  = the SECOND factorial moment of the block-occupancy indicator.
 So the covering bound is 'pairwise occupancy <= pairwise capacity', a pair-Pascal inequality;
 the binding-row obligation is the finite higher-Pascal dip (k=8,9). [ties HYP-3085 gK8, HYP-2716 Krawtchouk]
